Package: fancyfx
Title: Publication-Ready Effect and Evaluation Plots for Models
Version: 0.8.0
Authors@R:
    person("Camille", "Ross", email = "camille.ross@maine.edu", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-1428-2294"))
Description: Turns fitted models into figures ready for a manuscript. Plots the
    effect of a predictor with a rug of the raw data stacked directly above it,
    so a bend in the curve can be read against how much data supports it.
    Defaults are chosen for publication rather than for exploration: a clean
    theme with no grid or background panel, lettered panel labels, and a
    categorical palette checked for legibility under colour vision deficiency.
    Works across model types: generalized additive models fitted with 'mgcv' are
    shown as partial effects via 'gratia' -- including 'bam', 'gamm4', 'gamm'
    and shape-constrained 'scam' fits -- and other models, such as mixed and
    Bayesian ones, as predictions via 'marginaleffects'. Provides control
    over individual terms, factor-smooth interactions, several rug styles,
    variable transformations, and multi-panel layouts comparing either several
    predictors or several competing models. Also plots model evaluation for
    presence/absence models -- ROC curves with area under the curve, the true
    skill statistic against the decision threshold, calibration curves asking
    whether predicted probabilities are honest, and permutation importance --
    which require evaluation data explicitly and mark any figure scored on the
    data the model was fitted to. For spatial projections, maps the
    disagreement between ensemble members, flags where a projection leaves the
    range of conditions the model was fitted under, and aggregates rasters or
    point data into hexagonal bins. Includes several diagnostics that species
    distribution work relies on: spatial sorting bias in a train/test split,
    spatial thinning of unevenly surveyed records, and niche overlap between
    two predicted distributions with a randomisation test.
